  "step_id": "xub-grid-obstruction-review:directed-adf085d322254ea3a33183435c625e48:grid:v1",
  "content": "{\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"conditional_obstruction\",\n      \"argument\": \"Let t_q=2Lq, n=floor(R/(2L)), and X_q=S_{t_q}-beta*t_q. Writing a(z0)=log2(eta/z0), the event A={X_q<a(z0) for every q=1,...,n} makes every sampled coarse entry low. Since each useful block requires a nonlow entry, A implies N_C=0, independently of the pair-total and nonresonance requirements. Hence E[exp(-lambda N_C)] >= P(A). Therefore any uniform estimate P(A)>=c/n would imply E[exp(-lambda N_C)]>=c/n asymptotic to 2cL/R, obstructing a uniform C/R upper bound when L grows like c0 log R.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"actual_law_description\",\n      \"argument\": \"Under the uniform weak-composition law, equal full-block totals are exchangeable only after retaining the final remainder total. If a full block contains m=2L coordinates and the remainder has r=R-mn coordinates, then the joint mass of full-block totals u_1,...,u_n and remainder v, subject to sum u_q+v=K, is proportional to product_q binom(u_q+m-1,m-1) times binom(v+r-1,r-1), with the usual interpretation when r=0. Thus this is a weighted discrete bridge, not an iid walk.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"surrogate_warning\",\n      \"argument\": \"Cyclic-exchangeability or Brownian bridge heuristics do not by themselves prove P(A) asymptotic to 1/n. Even for exchangeable increments with negative total, the number of cyclic shifts whose partial sums remain negative need not equal one; repeated or periodic increments give elementary counterexamples. Consequently a cycle-lemma conclusion cannot be transferred without hypotheses tailored to this weighted composition bridge.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"endpoint_remainder_issue\",\n      \"argument\": \"Although the complete endpoint X_R=K-beta R has negative O(1) drift, the last full-grid endpoint X_{2Ln}=X_R-[(S_R-S_{2Ln})-beta r] need not have O(1) drift uniformly. The omitted remainder has length r<2L and random mass, so its centered contribution can be of order L or larger on individual compositions. Appending the true endpoint introduces a shorter, nonexchangeable block.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"threshold_and_ties\",\n      \"argument\": \"The barrier a(z0) varies over the fixed interval log2(eta/2)<a(z0)<log2(eta). Irrationality of beta prevents equality between two centered partial sums at distinct integer times, but it does not uniformly prevent a partial sum from equaling the externally chosen barrier for some z0. A valid estimate must specify strict versus weak crossing and remain uniform over z0, including barrier atoms.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"claim_status\": \"OPEN\",\n  \"first_missing_step\": \"Prove a uniform discrete ballot estimate for the actual weighted weak-composition bridge: for all admissible R,K, L=floor(c log R), remainders 0<=r<2L, and z0 in (1,2), establish at least P(S_{2Lq}-beta(2Lq)<log2(eta/z0) for every 1<=q<=n)>=c1/n, with c1>0 independent of all parameters, or determine the correct order. The theorem must handle the random remainder, negative O(1) complete endpoint, strict/weak barrier atoms, and possible repeated block totals. Without this estimate the proposed L/R obstruction is only conditional.\",\n  \"unexpected_findings\": [\n    {\n      \"escalation_candidate\": \"The O(1) drift of the complete endpoint does not pass to the last full coarse-grid point because of the omitted remainder.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"escalation_candidate\": \"A generic exchangeability/cycle-lemma argument is insufficient; negative endpoint and exchangeability alone do not force a 1/n persistence probability.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
  "reasoning": "**Considering cyclic exchangeability**\n\nI'm pondering whether cyclic exchangeability implies an argmax among pre-terminal partial sums uniformly. It seems there are complications with mapping shifts and argmax transforms, especially when correcting total values. But if I'm thinking about cyclically exchangeable increments, there might be a known maximum location uniformity. For a fixed negative sum, I wonder if the event where partial sums are less than or equal to zero doesn't always equal exactly 1/n. There are interesting nuances here!**Evaluating JSON status and steps**\n\nI’m considering the validity of JSON strings and arrays, and it seems the claim status is likely OPEN. I should also note that the first missing step is explicit, which could help guide my next actions. I want to keep an eye out for unexpected findings, especially when it comes to potential escalation candidates. My goal is to stay concise throughout this process.",
